is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The iptables/nftables FORWARD chain on router6 contains a firewall policy that drops all traffic forwarded from the eth2 interface (the r6_r3 link connecting router6 to router3 at 10.50.1.0/24).

**Mechanism:** The FORWARD chain has four rules anchored on `iifname "eth2"`: a narrow ACCEPT for TCP port 80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1, then blanket DROPs for TCP, UDP, and all remaining protocols (including ICMP). At the time of observation, 24 packets (2016 bytes) had already been silently dropped. Because all return/inbound cross-subnet traffic destined for the 172.18.51.0/24 subnet must re-enter router6 via eth2 from router3, it is silently discarded by these rules. The forward path (pc15 → external) leaves via eth2 and passes (eth1 is the ingress side, so no rule fires), but replies entering from router3 on eth2 are blocked before they can be forwarded to eth1 toward the 172.18.51.x hosts.

**Localization:** router6, eth2 (r6_r3 link, IP 10.50.1.2/24), FORWARD chain — iptables and nftables filter table both carry identical matching rules.

**Root cause:** A misconfigured ACL on router6 blocks all forwarded traffic ingressing from its router3-facing interface (eth2), making the entire 172.18.51.0/24 subnet (pc15–pc18) unreachable from and unable to reach any other subnet; DNS queries also fail since the DNS server is on a now-unreachable cross-subnet host, causing hostname-based file sharing between PC15 and PC16 to break even though their direct-IP path is intact.

**Fix:** Remove the three catch-all DROP rules from the nftables `FORWARD` chain (and their iptables equivalents) on router6 that match `iifname "eth2"`, retaining only the intended narrow policy if required.
=== END ===